  "name": "Gray-Scott Niches: spots meet worms",
  "description": "Two species of Gray-Scott Niches in the open, no walls: spots (left, F 0.0367 k 0.0649) and worms (right, F 0.058 k 0.065), each seeded as a few small spots and each living on its own food, so they compete only for space (interfere 0.3: where they overlap, each raises the other's death rate). They are evenly matched (in the lab, by territory: spots 68% at 20k steps, then 52%, 48% by 60k), so rather than one taking over, their fronts meet and they intermingle: dividing spots push in between the worms and worms thread through the spots. Tint = local mean feed (spots and worms feed differently); view b1 or b2 to see one species alone.",
